1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,600
Extreme heat, extreme cold, the unforgiving ocean,

2
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:11,740
the suffocating tropics, and the emptiness of space,

3
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:13,940
killer natural environments.

4
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,520
How can they be survived?

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:19,120
The science of staying alive.

6
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:22,520
Now, survival technology on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,500
Let's go.

8
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:49,860
Cold has been a killer of armies since the beginning of time.

9
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:55,600
200 years before the birth of Christ,

10
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:59,360
Hannibal marched over the Alps and lost more than 50,000 men

11
00:00:59,361 --> 00:01:02,600
and elephants to a snowstorm.

12
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:06,400
2,000 years later, the Russian winter wiped out half a million

13
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,800
of Napoleon's men as he retreated from Moscow.

14
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,040
Hitler's troops were stopped by the Russian winter too.

15
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,140
More than 100,000 German soldiers

16
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:19,220
suffered from cold injuries on the Russian front.

17
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:24,860
The United States armed forces weren't exempt from the cold

18
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:27,060
either.

19
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:30,800
Approximately 90,000 soldiers were classified as cold injuries

20
00:01:30,801 --> 00:01:32,000
during World War II.

21
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:38,440
The Korean War also produced many cold-related injuries.

22
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:42,060
Especially during the first winter

23
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:44,200
that our army was involved in that conflict,

24
00:01:44,560 --> 00:01:46,680
there was extremely low environmental temperatures

25
00:01:46,681 --> 00:01:49,200
in North Korea during that time,

26
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,960
especially up in the Chosan Reservoir.

27
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:54,920
And again, there were about 10,000 cold casualties

28
00:01:54,921 --> 00:01:56,020
during that conflict.

29
00:01:57,060 --> 00:02:01,860
One cold weather success story is the Native American Eskimo,

30
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,560
or Inuit.

31
00:02:04,660 --> 00:02:07,200
Skilled hunters of Arctic marine animals,

32
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:10,200
they have thrived for more than 15,000 years

33
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:14,180
in a land of bitter cold and a feeble sun that shines only

34
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:15,940
a few months of the year.

35
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:20,780
Over millennia, the Inuit had no choice

36
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:24,160
but to develop a cold survival lifestyle and technology.

37
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:29,120
They used animal skins for clothing,

38
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:33,460
because fur traps insulating air between the hairs.

39
00:02:33,560 --> 00:02:35,540
And they ate a diet rich in fats,

40
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:39,180
which provided excess calories to warm the body.

41
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,140
They lived almost exclusively on fat and meat

42
00:02:42,240 --> 00:02:44,720
and very little vegetable matter in their diet.

43
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:47,520
They boil the meat and dip the meat in the seal oil and eat it.

44
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:56,220
When white explorers came to the polar north,

45
00:02:56,221 --> 00:02:58,220
they tried to eat the native diet,

46
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,100
but found they couldn't digest it.

47
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,820
This made it hard to live and work in the Arctic,

48
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:06,000
unless you could bring your own food supply.

49
00:03:09,140 --> 00:03:11,260
In just about any extreme environment,

50
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,700
a well-balanced meal can be provided.

51
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:14,980
And you don't need flame.

52
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:18,380
All you need is water, and you're going to have a hot meal.

53
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:20,780
With a flameless, ready-to-eat meal,

54
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:23,320
a food pack is placed into a heat bag

55
00:03:23,420 --> 00:03:25,580
containing a magnesium wafer.

56
00:03:25,581 --> 00:03:30,580
water is poured into the bag onto the magnesium.

57
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:34,620
The magnesium heats up, creating steam, which in turn heats the

58
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:35,520
meal.

59
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:41,120
This flameless meal technology is available to the general public,

60
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:43,740
and you don't even need to add water.

61
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:47,240
Just pull a string, which breaks an internal water bladder.

62
00:03:47,340 --> 00:03:50,740
Wait a few minutes, and your meal is ready to eat.

63
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:59,820
Another major concern for the Inuit was eye damage.

64
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,220
In a land of snow, ice, sky, and water,

65
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:06,220
ultraviolet rays striking the eye from every angle

66
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:10,220
caused painful snow blindness.

67
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:13,240
To solve this problem, they developed a form of protective

68
00:04:13,340 --> 00:04:17,140
eyewear made of wood, bone, or hide strips

69
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:19,740
that had narrow slits cut into it.

70
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:23,340
The device was tied to the head, and the slits limited the amount

71
00:04:23,341 --> 00:04:28,080
limited the amount of light that reached the eye's retina.

72
00:04:28,180 --> 00:04:30,380
Today, we use a more sophisticated form of eye

73
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:31,180
protection.

74
00:04:34,880 --> 00:04:37,520
Modern sunglasses use a variety of techniques,

75
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:40,420
such as tinting and polarization, to eliminate

76
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:42,720
the amount and type of light that reaches the eye.

77
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,840
Most glare comes from horizontal surfaces,

78
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:50,240
like highways or frozen lakes, which

79
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:53,340
reflect light that is aligned in a horizontal direction.

80
00:04:53,341 --> 00:04:58,640
To combat this, light is filtered by polarized sunglasses

81
00:04:58,740 --> 00:05:00,840
because the surface of the lenses

82
00:05:00,940 --> 00:05:02,820
are coated with a chemical film that

83
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:07,080
creates a microscopic filter that will only allow light

84
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:10,320
through that is aligned in a vertical direction,

85
00:05:10,420 --> 00:05:12,460
knocking out the horizontal glare.

86
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:18,940
As white explorers continue to penetrate the polar regions,

87
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:21,460
their knowledge of how to survive in the cold

88
00:05:21,461 --> 00:05:24,760
increased substantially.

89
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:27,600
In the 1940s, the wind chill factor,

90
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,440
a popular phrase used by TV meteorologists

91
00:05:30,540 --> 00:05:32,580
to describe what the temperature and wind

92
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:35,500
feel like acting together, was first determined

93
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:39,380
by US Army Major Paul Sippel while on a trip to Antarctica.

94
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:45,220
To study how wind affects cooling potential,

95
00:05:45,320 --> 00:05:48,580
Sippel and his colleagues ingeniously set out tin food

96
00:05:48,581 --> 00:05:52,820
filled with water in the open polar air.

97
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:56,180
They measured how fast water would freeze based upon the air

98
00:05:56,280 --> 00:05:59,520
temperature, as well as how fast the wind was moving.

99
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:02,820
They came up with a formula to estimate the discomfort felt by

100
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:06,840
humans under various wind and temperature conditions.

101
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:11,640
If you put a person who's naked out in the environment,

102
00:06:11,740 --> 00:06:16,280
if there's no wind, we envelop basically a boundary of air

103
00:06:16,281 --> 00:06:19,980
of air over our skin that helps insulate us.

104
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:23,960
But when wind is placed on that skin area,

105
00:06:24,060 --> 00:06:28,560
it disturbs that boundary layer of air.

106
00:06:28,660 --> 00:06:32,100
The wind chill factor is a serious threat to survival.

107
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,240
But protection is easily found by wearing

108
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:36,240
a wind-resistant outer garment that

109
00:06:36,340 --> 00:06:39,240
prevents the disturbance of the warm microclimate air next

110
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:40,040
to the skin.

111
00:06:42,980 --> 00:06:45,780
Modern wind and water-resistant synthetic fabrics,

112
00:06:45,781 --> 00:06:49,580
like Gore-Tex have pores that are 20,000 times smaller

113
00:06:49,680 --> 00:06:52,160
than a water droplet and can readily

114
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,660
keep out blowing wind and rain.

115
00:06:54,760 --> 00:06:57,000
Additionally, these miracle fabrics

116
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:59,720
allow perspiration vapor to pass from the body

117
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:01,000
to the outside environment.

118
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:07,300
One of the most serious injuries

119
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:12,880
that can result from exposure to the cold is frostbite.

120
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:15,240
Since the 18th century, the Russian army

121
00:07:15,241 --> 00:07:18,180
recognized frostbite as a serious problem

122
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:21,840
and issued boots one size too large so that they could be

123
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:25,280
packed with straw during the winter.

124
00:07:25,380 --> 00:07:28,380
Frostbite is the actual freezing of tissue.

125
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:30,780
As an extremity begins to lose heat,

126
00:07:30,880 --> 00:07:34,460
the cells of the skin tissue shrink as water is drawn out

127
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:35,800
of them.

128
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:37,860
As the temperature continues to fall,

129
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:39,900
ice crystals suddenly form.

130
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:42,100
It's like a bomb going off in the tissue.

131
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:45,000
When that happens, it disrupts the tissues.

132
00:07:45,001 --> 00:07:46,500
especially the small blood vessels.

133
00:07:46,600 --> 00:07:49,300
So you lose the tubular integrity of the vascular

134
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:50,700
system.

135
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:52,700
Curiously, frostbite is a condition

136
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:56,120
the body imposes on itself, almost as a last ditch

137
00:07:56,220 --> 00:07:59,640
attempt to stay alive.

138
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:02,760
When exposed to the cold, the body's approach

139
00:08:02,860 --> 00:08:05,180
is to conserve your core heat at the expense

140
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:06,660
of your extremities.

141
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:09,000
So you shut off blood flow to your extremities,

142
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:11,260
and that blood goes to your core,

143
00:08:11,360 --> 00:08:15,000
and your central arterial pressure goes up.

144
00:08:15,001 --> 00:08:18,600
A frostbite injury can be as simple as blistering that

145
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:22,240
will heal and as serious as deep tissue freezing where

146
00:08:22,340 --> 00:08:25,740
extremities can be lost.

147
00:08:25,840 --> 00:08:29,040
The only effective way to treat all the degrees of frostbite

148
00:08:29,140 --> 00:08:34,460
is to rapidly rewarm the area by submerging it in hot water.

149
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:36,660
Of course, the best way to treat frostbite

150
00:08:36,760 --> 00:08:39,800
is to prevent it from happening in the first place.

151
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:41,660
In extreme cold weather environments,

152
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,760
keeping your hands warm is a must.

153
00:08:43,761 --> 00:08:46,460
is a must, it's real easy to do with the use of these little

154
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:47,560
heat packets.

155
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:50,360
They're lightweight, they're air activated,

156
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:55,000
and they slip real easily into your glove or your mitten.

157
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:58,700
There are two types of heat packs, a one-time use pack

158
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:00,880
that is activated by opening the package

159
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:04,340
and exposing its chemical contents to air,

160
00:09:04,440 --> 00:09:07,620
and a second type, a rechargeable liquid pack

161
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:10,880
that's set off by an internal metal catalyst that changes

162
00:09:10,881 --> 00:09:15,180
into a solid, giving off heat in the process.

163
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:17,180
Harsh environments like the Arctic

164
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:19,680
demand survival priorities.

165
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:23,060
Many people who are trapped in the cold, in severe cold,

166
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:25,000
their first issue is getting shelter,

167
00:09:25,100 --> 00:09:27,000
and their second issue is getting water.

168
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:30,160
Even with all the ice and snow present in the Arctic,

169
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:32,640
water is not readily available.

170
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:37,140
It must first be melted and warmed before being consumed.

171
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:39,600
After shelter and water, a rescue signal

172
00:09:39,601 --> 00:09:43,440
may be the next priority. Fire works very well.

173
00:09:43,540 --> 00:09:48,400
But with the snow, you can take contrasting items like pine bough,

174
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:51,340
logs, and you can set them out in the snow

175
00:09:51,440 --> 00:09:53,140
and set out your signaling device.

176
00:09:59,860 --> 00:10:02,320
But the newest and most high-tech of the search

177
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:07,520
and rescue signaling devices are handheld lasers.

178
00:10:07,521 --> 00:10:11,420
Effective day or night, these inexpensive eye-safe lasers

179
00:10:11,520 --> 00:10:15,420
use unique cylindrical lenses that generate a band of narrow

180
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:17,360
light.

181
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:20,360
From an aircraft, this light can be used to sweep miles

182
00:10:20,460 --> 00:10:23,460
of territory to search for a lost or injured person.

183
00:10:23,560 --> 00:10:25,980
But you're floating around in the middle of the ocean.

184
00:10:26,080 --> 00:10:27,880
Pilots can look out the window and go, hey,

185
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:30,980
there's a really strange green light.

186
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:33,820
The laser picks up a retro-reflective surface

187
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:36,280
and flashes brightly.

188
00:10:36,281 --> 00:10:38,920
The laser may also be used by a person on the ground

189
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:42,420
to sweep the air and signal rescue aircraft.

190
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,720
That's the only thing I know that you could catch

191
00:10:44,820 --> 00:10:47,720
the attention of a passenger at liner at 40,000 feet.

192
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:06,280
The ocean depth is unforgiving.

193
00:11:06,281 --> 00:11:10,420
Even powerful nuclear submarines like the Russian Kursk, which

194
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:15,520
sank in August of 2000, are not immune.

195
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:18,360
The United States Navy had its own tragedy,

196
00:11:18,460 --> 00:11:21,600
with the loss of the USS Thresher on a shakedown cruise

197
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,960
in the early 60s.

198
00:11:24,060 --> 00:11:27,100
Congress quickly charged the Navy to develop a comprehensive

199
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:30,640
survival system capable of rescuing submariners trapped

200
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:31,940
on the ocean floor.

201
00:11:31,941 --> 00:11:35,240
floor.

202
00:11:35,340 --> 00:11:40,180
The Avalon and the Mystic, twin 52-foot-long deep submergence

203
00:11:40,280 --> 00:11:44,180
rescue vehicles, or DSRVs, were the result.

204
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:52,100
Conjured from the space age technology of the 70s Apollo

205
00:11:52,200 --> 00:11:58,100
Moon program, these craft remain operational today.

206
00:11:58,200 --> 00:12:00,900
The Mystic and the Avalon have been in service for around 30

207
00:12:00,901 --> 00:12:02,400
over 30 years now.

208
00:12:02,500 --> 00:12:06,900
Over those three decades, we have made significant

209
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,900
improvements to its capabilities as far as search

210
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,740
and rescue and sonar.

211
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:17,660
The DSRVs can be flown anywhere in the world

212
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:21,660
at a moment's notice and can reach disabled submarines

213
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:26,560
in up to 2,000 feet of water.

214
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:29,760
Each rescue sub is made up of three high-strength steel

215
00:12:29,761 --> 00:12:32,000
enclosed in a tapered fiberglass body.

216
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:41,000
The forward sphere contains the pilot and copilot,

217
00:12:41,100 --> 00:12:42,500
and all the electronics.

218
00:12:51,420 --> 00:12:54,920
Two other crew members handle the middle and after sphere,

219
00:12:55,020 --> 00:12:58,360
which are used for transferring up to 24 rescued persons

220
00:12:58,361 --> 00:13:00,060
rescued persons from a downed sub.

221
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:06,400
If a submarine goes down, it may not always

222
00:13:06,500 --> 00:13:10,300
land upright on a flat, smooth bottom.

223
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:14,200
If the submarine has a 45-degree list or a 45-degree

224
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:17,820
up angle, the DSRV has the ability to position itself

225
00:13:17,920 --> 00:13:20,220
hovering over the submarine escape hatch

226
00:13:20,320 --> 00:13:23,920
and land on that hatch at that extreme angle

227
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,520
and still make them mate and bring the personnel on board.

228
00:13:28,360 --> 00:13:31,460
But what does the future hold for the more than 30-year-old

229
00:13:31,461 --> 00:13:33,740
Avalon and Mystic?

230
00:13:33,741 --> 00:13:42,440
We expect to phase into a more modern surface-operated system.

231
00:13:42,441 --> 00:13:44,200
Though piloted from the surface,

232
00:13:44,201 --> 00:13:48,580
the new craft will still have one human operator on board.

233
00:13:48,581 --> 00:13:50,580
It will also be easier to mate to the hatch

234
00:13:50,581 --> 00:13:53,560
of a disabled submarine.

235
00:13:53,561 --> 00:13:55,220
The new system uses an articulated skirt

236
00:13:55,221 --> 00:13:58,100
so the rescue vehicle stays at a zero attitude

237
00:13:58,101 --> 00:14:01,700
while the skirt itself, which is actually a mating surface,

238
00:14:01,701 --> 00:14:04,880
rolls according to many joints hydraulically

239
00:14:04,881 --> 00:14:06,100
and mates to the submarine.

240
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:14,340
A diver wearing a hard suit, something

241
00:14:14,341 --> 00:14:16,720
that looks like a futuristic space suit,

242
00:14:16,721 --> 00:14:20,760
will accompany the new rescue sub.

243
00:14:20,761 --> 00:14:23,060
Our suit can go down to 2,000 feet.

244
00:14:23,061 --> 00:14:25,220
He will be there, the first one on the scene

245
00:14:25,221 --> 00:14:27,620
of a disabled submarine to assess the submarine.

246
00:14:30,420 --> 00:14:32,360
The hard suit is really a mini sub

247
00:14:32,460 --> 00:14:33,660
with articulated joints.

248
00:14:37,460 --> 00:14:40,160
The operator sits on a bicycle-like seat

249
00:14:40,260 --> 00:14:43,080
and steers with foot pedals connected to variable pitched

250
00:14:43,180 --> 00:14:43,880
propellers.

251
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:51,320
The diver is able to move through the water column virtually

252
00:14:51,420 --> 00:14:54,220
at will.

253
00:14:54,221 --> 00:14:57,820
Importantly, the hard suit keeps the diver in a one atmosphere

254
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:01,720
environment, protecting him from decompression sickness,

255
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:05,100
commonly known to scuba divers as the bends.

256
00:15:08,540 --> 00:15:12,140
When a diver descends, the pressure

257
00:15:12,240 --> 00:15:15,100
causes any gas that you are breathing

258
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:18,740
to be absorbed into your body at a rapid rate.

259
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:21,380
The problem arises when you leave the bottom

260
00:15:21,381 --> 00:15:24,880
and start back to the surface.

261
00:15:24,980 --> 00:15:28,660
If you do it slowly, the gas leaves your body

262
00:15:28,760 --> 00:15:32,560
and you breathe this excess gas out with no problem.

263
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:37,160
If you very rapidly come back to the surface

264
00:15:37,260 --> 00:15:39,400
and release the pressure, you form

265
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:42,560
bubbles in the liquid of your body,

266
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:45,600
and those bubbles give you a lot of problems.

267
00:15:45,700 --> 00:15:47,940
For over a century, a cure for the bends

268
00:15:48,040 --> 00:15:50,680
has been to place the diver in a decompression chamber

269
00:15:50,681 --> 00:15:52,920
upon surfacing.

270
00:15:53,020 --> 00:15:55,160
The first decompression chambers were developed

271
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:58,560
in the late 1800s for workers tunneling under rivers

272
00:15:58,660 --> 00:16:00,260
like the Thames and the East River.

273
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:05,960
Even though they weren't submerged in water,

274
00:16:06,060 --> 00:16:08,260
the workers were still breathing regular air

275
00:16:08,360 --> 00:16:11,100
at great pressure, effectively forcing nitrogen

276
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:13,740
into their bloodstream.

277
00:16:13,840 --> 00:16:15,840
Today, portable decompression chambers

278
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:18,500
are the latest survival gear to treat divers stricken

279
00:16:18,600 --> 00:16:20,680
with the bends.

280
00:16:20,681 --> 00:16:23,180
Lightweight enough to be carried by two people,

281
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:27,080
a polyurethane gas bag is enclosed in cargo netting

282
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:30,260
and capped with two aluminum ends.

283
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:32,820
The diver is placed inside the chamber, which

284
00:16:32,920 --> 00:16:36,800
is then pressurized back to the depth they were diving at.

285
00:16:36,900 --> 00:16:39,700
The pressure is then slowly decreased over time

286
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:42,560
to allow the accumulated gas to be released from the blood.

287
00:16:45,380 --> 00:16:47,040
The chamber can also be carried off

288
00:16:47,041 --> 00:16:49,880
to a medical facility in case further treatment is needed.

289
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:56,480
Technology has not only helped people live under the sea,

290
00:16:56,580 --> 00:16:59,460
it has also allowed us to survive on the ocean's surface.

291
00:17:02,420 --> 00:17:06,760
When the Titanic struck an iceberg on April 12, 1912,

292
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:10,400
over 1,500 people lost their lives.

293
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:13,240
Many of the casualties were due to hypothermia

294
00:17:13,340 --> 00:17:15,200
and not drowning.

295
00:17:15,201 --> 00:17:18,300
Hypothermia essentially is a condition that happens

296
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:21,740
when the environment is such that the body loses more heat

297
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:26,740
to that environment than what we can produce.

298
00:17:26,840 --> 00:17:29,180
As the core body temperature drops,

299
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:31,780
the victim becomes stuporous.

300
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:34,560
As more heat is lost, coma can follow.

301
00:17:38,160 --> 00:17:40,120
Passengers from the Titanic probably

302
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:43,920
died within an hour after entering the water.

303
00:17:43,921 --> 00:17:46,260
If the Titanic were to be rebuilt today,

304
00:17:46,360 --> 00:17:49,460
she would carry a modern lifeboat system.

305
00:17:49,560 --> 00:17:52,360
At the push of a button, a life raft package

306
00:17:52,460 --> 00:17:58,260
leaps overboard, trailing behind an enclosed sliding tube.

307
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:01,080
Upon hitting the water, the raft inflates,

308
00:18:01,180 --> 00:18:04,080
and passengers drop down the slide completely protected

309
00:18:04,180 --> 00:18:06,680
from the elements.

310
00:18:06,780 --> 00:18:10,420
Another piece of modern gear used to survive exposure in the sea

311
00:18:10,421 --> 00:18:13,760
is a cold-water immersion suit.

312
00:18:13,860 --> 00:18:16,420
During the late 20s, the US Navy started

313
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:19,460
to produce combination flotation and exposure suits

314
00:18:19,560 --> 00:18:20,760
for pilots.

315
00:18:20,860 --> 00:18:23,060
But true immersion suits weren't fully developed

316
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:26,760
until after World War II.

317
00:18:26,860 --> 00:18:29,880
Today, the Mustang Ocean Commander,

318
00:18:29,980 --> 00:18:35,120
with its watertight zippers and seams, hood and face seal,

319
00:18:35,220 --> 00:18:38,820
and inflatable head pillow, is the state of the art

320
00:18:38,821 --> 00:18:40,520
in cold water immersion gear.

321
00:18:47,620 --> 00:18:50,460
The jungle is an extreme environment,

322
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:53,260
filled with poisonous insects, oppressive heat,

323
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:54,360
and dense vegetation.

324
00:18:57,280 --> 00:19:00,880
To stay alive in the jungle takes skill and knowledge.

325
00:19:00,980 --> 00:19:03,920
And interestingly, some of the most effective survival

326
00:19:04,020 --> 00:19:05,380
technology is ancient.

327
00:19:05,381 --> 00:19:08,280
ancient.

328
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:10,820
For decades, native Negrito instructors

329
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:13,620
taught jungle survival techniques at the Subic Bay

330
00:19:13,720 --> 00:19:15,320
Naval Base in the Philippines.

331
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:24,440
Members of an ancient race of pygmies,

332
00:19:24,540 --> 00:19:26,640
Negrito tribesmen survived by learning

333
00:19:26,740 --> 00:19:31,080
to use the jungle for food, medicine, and weapons.

334
00:19:31,180 --> 00:19:33,180
They taught the Americans how to make fire

335
00:19:33,181 --> 00:19:36,680
method of rubbing two sticks together.

336
00:19:36,780 --> 00:19:38,780
A good snake bite remedy is the juice

337
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:41,580
from a particular jungle grass.

338
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:43,620
As bamboo is plentiful in the jungle,

339
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:47,620
it can make a quick rain shelter or a bowl for carrying water.

340
00:19:53,140 --> 00:19:55,840
One of the more terrifying aspects of the jungle

341
00:19:55,940 --> 00:19:58,740
is that it is filled with mosquitoes carrying deadly

342
00:19:58,840 --> 00:19:59,980
malaria.

343
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:02,300
In the late 1800s, it was discovered

344
00:20:02,301 --> 00:20:05,200
it was discovered that malaria was caused by a microscopic

345
00:20:05,201 --> 00:20:08,200
protozoan injected into the blood from the bite

346
00:20:08,201 --> 00:20:11,800
of an infected mosquito.

347
00:20:11,801 --> 00:20:14,080
During the building of the Panama Canal,

348
00:20:14,081 --> 00:20:16,380
doctors learned to control the spread of malaria

349
00:20:16,381 --> 00:20:18,420
by destroying the breeding grounds of mosquitoes.

350
00:20:22,360 --> 00:20:24,680
In the Pacific during World War II,

351
00:20:24,681 --> 00:20:28,260
malaria-carrying mosquitoes were considered the number two enemy

352
00:20:28,261 --> 00:20:31,300
after the Japanese.

353
00:20:31,301 --> 00:20:33,060
As the American forces fought their way

354
00:20:33,160 --> 00:20:36,480
through the South Pacific, mosquito eradication programs

355
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:40,080
went hand in hand with rifle and bullet.

356
00:20:40,180 --> 00:20:42,980
Swamps were drained whenever possible.

357
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:47,580
DDT was sprayed on standing pools and in native huts.

358
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:49,920
In the past, if you caught malaria,

359
00:20:50,020 --> 00:20:52,980
quinine was the only effective means to treat it.

360
00:20:53,080 --> 00:20:55,800
One of the four alkaloids found in the bark of the cinchona

361
00:20:55,900 --> 00:20:59,860
tree, quinine was used by native populations in South America

362
00:20:59,861 --> 00:21:03,260
than 400 years and was introduced into Europe

363
00:21:03,360 --> 00:21:04,660
by Jesuit missionaries.

364
00:21:08,760 --> 00:21:11,800
The British used quinine extensively in India,

365
00:21:11,900 --> 00:21:14,380
and it was the basis for their famous gin and tonics

366
00:21:14,480 --> 00:21:17,520
in the afternoon.

367
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:21,140
The drug Adabrin replaced quinine in the 1930s

368
00:21:21,240 --> 00:21:24,320
and was used effectively on the troops in the South Pacific

369
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:26,420
to greatly reduce the incidence of malaria.

370
00:21:29,860 --> 00:21:33,460
Today, adabrine is considered to have too many side effects

371
00:21:33,560 --> 00:21:35,600
and is no longer used.

372
00:21:35,700 --> 00:21:38,340
A number of drugs have been developed to treat malaria,

373
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:43,340
but malaria strains have quickly become resistant to them.

374
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:45,640
Ultimately, the best cure for malaria

375
00:21:45,740 --> 00:21:47,240
is not to get bitten.

376
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:49,880
Your best bet for protecting yourself

377
00:21:49,980 --> 00:21:52,520
from insects in a jungle environment

378
00:21:52,620 --> 00:21:55,220
or any environment that's infested with insects

379
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:57,520
is to protect your skin with clothing.

380
00:21:57,521 --> 00:21:59,520
You've got gloves, put gloves on.

381
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:01,120
Long sleeves, roll them down.

382
00:22:05,620 --> 00:22:09,260
Some insects, like mosquitoes, are clearly deadly.

383
00:22:09,360 --> 00:22:12,560
But other insects can be eaten for food.

384
00:22:12,660 --> 00:22:15,580
Ants can be useful if you are hurt and immobilized in the

385
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:19,580
jungle, definitely a low-tech survival method.

386
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:24,140
If you're laying there and you're sweating,

387
00:22:24,240 --> 00:22:27,280
you're probably going to have a lot of ants close or near

388
00:22:27,281 --> 00:22:32,280
nearby your area, so you can reach right out and grab one.

389
00:22:32,380 --> 00:22:34,180
Now, you don't just want to pop it in your mouth.

390
00:22:34,280 --> 00:22:37,420
You want to actually squeeze the head and then put it in your

391
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:39,320
mouth and just eat it right up.

392
00:22:39,420 --> 00:22:40,620
Tastes like sweet tart.

393
00:22:42,720 --> 00:22:45,260
One of the most basic and dangerous hazards of the

394
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:49,200
jungle is getting lost.

395
00:22:49,300 --> 00:22:51,700
Navigating in the jungle is very, very difficult.

396
00:22:51,800 --> 00:22:54,200
Before you actually move, you've got to take into a lot of

397
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:55,900
different considerations.

398
00:22:55,901 --> 00:22:57,900
The capabilities of your group.

399
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,140
Do they have the means or the capabilities

400
00:23:00,240 --> 00:23:02,400
of physical conditioning to make the movement?

401
00:23:02,500 --> 00:23:03,800
Are they hurt?

402
00:23:03,900 --> 00:23:06,040
If you have a compass, use your compass.

403
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:07,780
Once you know north, you're going

404
00:23:07,880 --> 00:23:11,280
to be able to start orienting yourself as far as which

405
00:23:11,380 --> 00:23:12,680
direction to move and why.

406
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:20,300
Early compasses were developed in China around 100 AD.

407
00:23:20,400 --> 00:23:23,600
They used lodestones, iron oxide minerals,

408
00:23:23,601 --> 00:23:25,600
as a magnetic element that would point north.

409
00:23:29,100 --> 00:23:31,340
Between the 7th and 12th century,

410
00:23:31,440 --> 00:23:34,140
lodestones were replaced by magnetic needles,

411
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:36,240
and compasses became common on ships.

412
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:42,180
Around the late 1800s, liquid-filled casings were

413
00:23:42,280 --> 00:23:43,560
perfected.

414
00:23:43,660 --> 00:23:46,120
The liquid dampens the needle's oscillations

415
00:23:46,220 --> 00:23:49,100
as it swings into alignment with the Earth's magnetic field.

416
00:23:49,101 --> 00:23:54,100
The compass has been a trusted survival tool for centuries,

417
00:23:54,200 --> 00:23:57,740
but the ideal piece of high-tech orientation gear

418
00:23:57,840 --> 00:24:00,540
may be the GPS.

419
00:24:00,640 --> 00:24:03,740
GPS stands for Global Positioning System.

420
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:07,460
It's a constellation of 24 satellites that are created

421
00:24:07,560 --> 00:24:10,160
and maintained by the United States government,

422
00:24:10,260 --> 00:24:12,760
and it allows anyone with a receiver

423
00:24:12,860 --> 00:24:16,260
that can listen to GPS signals to locate themselves very

424
00:24:16,360 --> 00:24:17,800
accurately on the Earth.

425
00:24:17,801 --> 00:24:22,400
on the Earth. In the early 1970s,

426
00:24:22,401 --> 00:24:25,340
the Department of Defense developed the system so it could

427
00:24:25,341 --> 00:24:28,340
accurately locate troops and assets on the battlefield.

428
00:24:31,740 --> 00:24:35,660
The satellites travel in orbits 11,000 miles above the Earth

429
00:24:35,661 --> 00:24:39,960
while continuously sending out a radio transmission.

430
00:24:39,961 --> 00:24:43,860
A GPS receiver needs to have at least three satellites in order

431
00:24:43,861 --> 00:24:48,500
to calculate a position fix. It uses a mathematical function

432
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:54,000
called triangulation in order to take multiple positions and

433
00:24:54,100 --> 00:24:57,900
calculate where you're starting from, essentially.

434
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,920
When the global positioning system became operational,

435
00:25:01,020 --> 00:25:05,020
it provided two signals, one for the military and one for the

436
00:25:05,120 --> 00:25:08,760
general public. The signal used by the public was

437
00:25:08,860 --> 00:25:12,560
intentionally degraded to protect US security interests

438
00:25:12,561 --> 00:25:15,460
accurate to within about 300 feet.

439
00:25:15,560 --> 00:25:18,860
But since the year 2000, this practice has stopped,

440
00:25:18,960 --> 00:25:21,200
and the signal is accurate to within inches.

441
00:25:24,100 --> 00:25:26,600
Sometimes survival depends on knowing where you are in the

442
00:25:26,700 --> 00:25:28,000
jungle.

443
00:25:28,100 --> 00:25:31,320
Other times it's letting someone else know where you are.

444
00:25:31,420 --> 00:25:34,120
You need to get exposure to the sky, because nine times

445
00:25:34,220 --> 00:25:38,260
out of 10, you're going to get rescued by some sort of helicopter

446
00:25:38,360 --> 00:25:41,260
or by air.

447
00:25:41,261 --> 00:25:43,760
Ultimately, because of the heavy canopy,

448
00:25:43,860 --> 00:25:46,300
the best signaling device to have in the jungle

449
00:25:46,400 --> 00:25:50,000
is a 406 megahertz emergency locator beacon.

450
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:55,900
The beacon sends out a radio signal,

451
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:00,720
which is picked up by satellites orbiting the Earth.

452
00:26:00,820 --> 00:26:03,920
The satellites are operated by various international agencies

453
00:26:04,020 --> 00:26:06,620
as part of a worldwide distress system

454
00:26:06,720 --> 00:26:11,260
to listen for emergency calls from activated beacons.

455
00:26:11,261 --> 00:26:15,060
Most airplanes and ships have them.

456
00:26:15,061 --> 00:26:17,460
If a plane crashes or a ship sinks,

457
00:26:17,461 --> 00:26:20,460
the beacons are designed to activate automatically.

458
00:26:20,461 --> 00:26:25,500
When a beacon is turned on, it starts to transmit a signal

459
00:26:25,501 --> 00:26:29,500
with a beacon-unique identifying code.

460
00:26:29,501 --> 00:26:31,900
A satellite immediately detects the signal

461
00:26:31,901 --> 00:26:35,420
and determines the distress position.

462
00:26:35,421 --> 00:26:38,660
It also checks the identifying code against a registration

463
00:26:38,661 --> 00:26:41,860
a registration database to determine who and what

464
00:26:41,960 --> 00:26:43,800
is in trouble.

465
00:26:43,900 --> 00:26:46,500
A distress alert is then routed to the proper rescue

466
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:47,400
authorities.

467
00:26:52,200 --> 00:26:57,040
When Saddam Hussein invaded Kuwait on August 2, 1990,

468
00:26:57,140 --> 00:27:00,120
the United States responded by dropping more than a half

469
00:27:00,220 --> 00:27:04,920
million troops into the harsh desert of Saudi Arabia.

470
00:27:05,020 --> 00:27:08,660
In the desert, water is the first line of defense.

471
00:27:08,661 --> 00:27:11,300
Soldiers under stress in desert battle conditions

472
00:27:11,400 --> 00:27:14,700
usually don't drink enough water.

473
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:19,240
They don't feel thirsty, even though they are dehydrated.

474
00:27:19,340 --> 00:27:22,640
Heat casualties are the result.

475
00:27:22,740 --> 00:27:26,280
The body needs at least four quarts of water a day

476
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:27,380
to survive.

477
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:29,380
We teach drink six to eight.

478
00:27:29,480 --> 00:27:32,220
That way, they'll at least drink four quarts of water.

479
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:36,280
General society is usually dehydrated,

480
00:27:36,281 --> 00:27:40,880
and they're used to living in a dehydrated state.

481
00:27:40,980 --> 00:27:43,760
In the desert, a person needs at least one and a half

482
00:27:43,860 --> 00:27:48,900
quarts of water per hour to remain fully functional.

483
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,520
During the Gulf War, US armed forces

484
00:27:51,620 --> 00:27:55,860
consumed more than four million gallons of water per day.

485
00:27:55,960 --> 00:27:57,960
But what if you don't have any water?

486
00:27:58,060 --> 00:27:59,940
How do you find it?

487
00:28:00,040 --> 00:28:02,640
Your best bet to find water in the desert

488
00:28:02,641 --> 00:28:05,980
is to go to old stream beds and dig down.

489
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:08,440
Now there's things that we call incidental water,

490
00:28:08,540 --> 00:28:11,480
which early in the morning condensation forms

491
00:28:11,580 --> 00:28:14,180
on the various vegetation around.

492
00:28:14,280 --> 00:28:17,580
So you could tie cloth around your legs

493
00:28:17,680 --> 00:28:19,600
and walk through it and gather it.

494
00:28:19,700 --> 00:28:21,700
You could leave your socks out at night,

495
00:28:21,800 --> 00:28:23,100
your T-shirt out at night.

496
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:26,000
Condensation forms on it, and you can wring it out,

497
00:28:26,100 --> 00:28:28,540
and you can drink that water.

498
00:28:28,640 --> 00:28:30,200
If you were lucky enough to find water

499
00:28:30,201 --> 00:28:32,540
that's not produced by condensation,

500
00:28:32,640 --> 00:28:34,100
you can't just drink it.

501
00:28:34,200 --> 00:28:36,940
It needs to be purified and disinfected

502
00:28:37,040 --> 00:28:39,340
to get rid of harmful microorganisms

503
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:44,340
like Giardia and Cryptosporidium.

504
00:28:44,440 --> 00:28:46,240
To properly filter your water, you're going to need

505
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:49,860
some sort of filtration system, such as this straw.

506
00:28:49,960 --> 00:28:52,060
Some things you're looking for in your filtration system

507
00:28:52,160 --> 00:28:57,000
is it needs to be a 0.03 micron opening in the filter itself

508
00:28:57,100 --> 00:29:00,200
or smaller, or it needs to have some sort of chemical

509
00:29:00,201 --> 00:29:03,800
such as iodine or betadine.

510
00:29:03,900 --> 00:29:06,300
It's not only bacteria and microorganisms

511
00:29:06,400 --> 00:29:08,340
that can make water undrinkable.

512
00:29:08,440 --> 00:29:12,140
Salt in water is a huge problem, especially in deserts

513
00:29:12,240 --> 00:29:15,540
surrounded by seawater.

514
00:29:15,640 --> 00:29:17,860
Special saltwater filtering systems

515
00:29:17,960 --> 00:29:21,560
using a reverse osmosis process were originally developed for the

516
00:29:21,660 --> 00:29:25,700
military in the 1980s.

517
00:29:25,800 --> 00:29:28,560
Water is forced at high pressure through a membrane

518
00:29:28,561 --> 00:29:32,960
that is impermeable to salt molecules, as well as pathogens

519
00:29:33,060 --> 00:29:39,400
such as microorganisms, cysts, bacteria, and viruses.

520
00:29:39,500 --> 00:29:43,400
Only 10% of the salt water passes through and ends up fresh.

521
00:29:46,520 --> 00:29:48,920
Once a supply of water is secured,

522
00:29:49,020 --> 00:29:52,020
protection from the burning sun is the second line of defense

523
00:29:52,120 --> 00:29:55,220
for surviving in the desert.

524
00:29:55,320 --> 00:29:57,720
The Bedouins of Saudi Arabia, who

525
00:29:57,721 --> 00:30:01,720
lineage back to biblical times have long known that clothing

526
00:30:01,820 --> 00:30:05,020
can mean the difference between life and death.

527
00:30:05,120 --> 00:30:07,360
As part of their survival technology,

528
00:30:07,460 --> 00:30:10,260
they developed a layering system that kept them alive

529
00:30:10,360 --> 00:30:11,860
in the harsh, burning environment.

530
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:18,880
The flowing road of the Bedouins allows insulation.

531
00:30:18,980 --> 00:30:23,280
Clothing actually can keep one cool in a desert environment

532
00:30:23,380 --> 00:30:26,640
because there are air layers within the different layers

533
00:30:26,641 --> 00:30:28,440
of clothing.

534
00:30:28,540 --> 00:30:30,580
The more layers of cloth and air,

535
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:34,180
the cooler the body remains.

536
00:30:34,280 --> 00:30:37,920
The US military's battle dress uniform, or BDU,

537
00:30:38,020 --> 00:30:41,080
used during the Gulf War, works on a different principle

538
00:30:41,180 --> 00:30:42,560
than the robes of a Bedouin.

539
00:30:45,660 --> 00:30:48,760
Made of a single cloth woven of nylon and cotton,

540
00:30:48,860 --> 00:30:51,660
the BDU depends on the evaporative cooling of sweat

541
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:55,100
from the skin rather than trapped air insulation

542
00:30:55,101 --> 00:30:56,500
to keep a body cool.

543
00:31:00,500 --> 00:31:03,040
If one has unlimited water resources,

544
00:31:03,140 --> 00:31:05,340
the battle dress uniform is an effective way

545
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:08,540
to dress in the desert.

546
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:13,060
That's because sweating is the major way the body keeps cool.

547
00:31:13,160 --> 00:31:17,160
We have a large surface area over which to dissipate heat.

548
00:31:17,260 --> 00:31:19,260
There'll be over 3 million sweat glands

549
00:31:19,360 --> 00:31:21,000
on a normal human body.

550
00:31:21,100 --> 00:31:24,260
You can secrete quite a bit of sweat.

551
00:31:24,261 --> 00:31:27,360
Sweating does cool the body, but sometimes this heat

552
00:31:27,460 --> 00:31:30,360
exchange can use a little help, especially if you're

553
00:31:30,460 --> 00:31:33,100
fighting your way across the Iraqi desert inside an Abrams

554
00:31:33,200 --> 00:31:36,400
M1A1 tank.

555
00:31:36,500 --> 00:31:38,500
In this pressure cooker environment,

556
00:31:38,600 --> 00:31:42,820
tank crews need extra protection from the heat.

557
00:31:42,920 --> 00:31:45,420
One Gulf War solution was found in the concept

558
00:31:45,520 --> 00:31:47,520
of a microclimate.

559
00:31:47,620 --> 00:31:50,820
The microclimate cooling system is composed of a vest that

560
00:31:50,821 --> 00:31:54,620
that receives air off of a blower in the tank.

561
00:31:54,720 --> 00:31:55,920
It's cooled air.

562
00:31:56,020 --> 00:31:59,560
And so each individual actually has his own air conditioner

563
00:31:59,660 --> 00:32:04,460
in a vest underneath his protective clothing.

564
00:32:04,560 --> 00:32:05,900
The ordinary person, of course,

565
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:07,740
isn't likely to have a desert wardrobe

566
00:32:07,840 --> 00:32:10,800
equipped with its own cooling system.

567
00:32:10,900 --> 00:32:13,680
So what can the typical denizen of the air-conditioned mall

568
00:32:13,780 --> 00:32:15,880
do to survive?

569
00:32:15,980 --> 00:32:18,320
If you found yourself lost in the desert,

570
00:32:18,420 --> 00:32:20,180
probably the first thing you need to think about

571
00:32:20,181 --> 00:32:22,920
is some type of signal, because you want to get out of there

572
00:32:23,020 --> 00:32:26,420
as fast as possible.

573
00:32:26,520 --> 00:32:28,420
If it's daytime, a signal mirror

574
00:32:28,520 --> 00:32:32,120
can be seen for more than 70 miles.

575
00:32:32,220 --> 00:32:34,520
At night, three evenly spaced fires

576
00:32:34,620 --> 00:32:36,420
are the international distress signal,

577
00:32:36,520 --> 00:32:37,340
and will summon help.

578
00:32:41,240 --> 00:32:46,180
Glow lights, or chem sticks, are excellent for signaling.

579
00:32:46,280 --> 00:32:48,900
Light is given off by a chemical reaction, which

580
00:32:48,901 --> 00:32:51,240
which takes place when a solution of glucose

581
00:32:51,241 --> 00:32:53,640
is slowly mixed in with an alcoholic alkaline.

582
00:32:57,140 --> 00:33:00,180
A glow light can last for hours, and if twirled at the end

583
00:33:00,181 --> 00:33:01,940
of a string, can be seen for miles.

584
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:07,420
You need to conserve energy first and foremost.

585
00:33:07,421 --> 00:33:08,720
Undo haste makes waste.

586
00:33:08,721 --> 00:33:11,180
In other words, work smarter, not harder.

587
00:33:11,181 --> 00:33:14,560
And that's going to set you up first and foremost for success.

588
00:33:14,561 --> 00:33:19,700
The exploration of space, especially long duration

589
00:33:19,800 --> 00:33:21,900
missions like to the planet Mars,

590
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:25,300
presents a complex extreme environment for Earth-bound

591
00:33:25,400 --> 00:33:28,200
life forms like man.

592
00:33:28,300 --> 00:33:32,220
Solar and cosmic radiation, micrometeorites,

593
00:33:32,320 --> 00:33:37,820
the absence of gravity, the list of problems is formidable.

594
00:33:37,920 --> 00:33:41,060
Not to mention the fact that in order to even get into space,

595
00:33:41,061 --> 00:33:45,400
you have to hit an escape speed of over 17,000 miles per hour

596
00:33:45,500 --> 00:33:47,000
atop a chemical burning rocket.

597
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:58,280
Early aviation pioneers like daredevil flyer Wiley Post

598
00:33:58,380 --> 00:34:01,280
knew that in order to survive in the upper atmosphere,

599
00:34:01,380 --> 00:34:05,380
special technology had to be developed.

600
00:34:05,480 --> 00:34:08,820
Post designed and used the first pressurized flight suit

601
00:34:08,821 --> 00:34:12,160
suit in the early 30s.

602
00:34:12,260 --> 00:34:14,520
When it was inflated, it went rigid,

603
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:18,660
making it impossible to bend at the waist.

604
00:34:18,760 --> 00:34:20,960
The first space suit for the Mercury capsule

605
00:34:21,060 --> 00:34:23,060
wasn't really a true space suit.

606
00:34:23,160 --> 00:34:27,480
It was a modified high-altitude pressure suit.

607
00:34:27,580 --> 00:34:29,940
As space suits developed, they incrementally

608
00:34:30,040 --> 00:34:32,640
allowed more mobility, ultimately permitting

609
00:34:32,740 --> 00:34:36,640
astronauts to stoop down and pick up moon rocks.

610
00:34:36,641 --> 00:34:38,640
But you need more than a space suit,

611
00:34:38,740 --> 00:34:41,320
or the protection of a spacecraft, for that matter,

612
00:34:41,420 --> 00:34:43,320
to survive in space.

613
00:34:43,420 --> 00:34:45,320
The human body is profoundly affected

614
00:34:45,420 --> 00:34:48,320
by two conditions of space travel, weightlessness

615
00:34:48,420 --> 00:34:50,180
and cosmic radiation.

616
00:34:50,280 --> 00:34:51,960
Each is a challenge.

617
00:34:52,060 --> 00:34:55,960
We grow up using gravity, and we grow up developing mechanisms

618
00:34:56,060 --> 00:34:58,000
to deal with gravity.

619
00:34:58,100 --> 00:35:00,200
When we're in weightlessness and we no longer have

620
00:35:00,300 --> 00:35:04,400
the gravitational stresses, things become changed.

621
00:35:04,401 --> 00:35:08,140
and they're changed not for the better.

622
00:35:08,240 --> 00:35:10,540
The two very serious and obvious dangers

623
00:35:10,640 --> 00:35:13,940
of prolonged weightlessness are bone and muscle loss.

624
00:35:14,040 --> 00:35:16,140
We rebuild bone every time we walk

625
00:35:16,240 --> 00:35:17,980
and every time we take a step because we're

626
00:35:18,080 --> 00:35:21,120
supporting our body weight against the forces of gravity.

627
00:35:21,220 --> 00:35:23,460
And those compressive forces on the bone

628
00:35:23,560 --> 00:35:26,360
allow us to take calcium out of the bloodstream,

629
00:35:26,460 --> 00:35:27,860
build up new bone.

630
00:35:27,960 --> 00:35:32,800
The old bone is appropriately removed and goes back

631
00:35:32,900 --> 00:35:34,400
into the bloodstream.

632
00:35:34,401 --> 00:35:37,300
In zero gravity, the compressive forces on the body

633
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:38,900
are negligible.

634
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:41,440
And after time, the weight-bearing bones

635
00:35:41,540 --> 00:35:44,380
become thin and fragile.

636
00:35:44,480 --> 00:35:47,080
The weight-bearing muscles also suffer.

637
00:35:47,180 --> 00:35:51,580
The big muscles in our legs, the muscles that support us

638
00:35:51,680 --> 00:35:56,520
in standing upright, are the ones that we find dramatically

639
00:35:56,620 --> 00:36:02,060
reduced, not only in size, but also in strength.

640
00:36:02,061 --> 00:36:04,300
Arm muscles, interestingly, are less affected,

641
00:36:04,400 --> 00:36:06,660
as they are the prime means used for locomotion

642
00:36:06,760 --> 00:36:08,000
in a weightless environment.

643
00:36:12,100 --> 00:36:14,100
During a year-long space mission,

644
00:36:14,200 --> 00:36:16,800
a young astronaut could end up with the bone and muscle

645
00:36:16,900 --> 00:36:19,720
structure of a 70-year-old if no preventative measures

646
00:36:19,820 --> 00:36:20,720
are taken.

647
00:36:22,820 --> 00:36:25,420
Vigorous exercise is one countermeasure

648
00:36:25,520 --> 00:36:28,520
used on board the space shuttle and the International Space

649
00:36:28,620 --> 00:36:29,920
Station.

650
00:36:29,921 --> 00:36:33,520
How do you use a treadmill in space?

651
00:36:33,521 --> 00:36:39,740
You have to be kept down somehow, and so there is a system of harnesses that look like a window washer's harness

652
00:36:39,741 --> 00:36:45,880
and a bungee cord, elastic cords, that hold you down to the treadmill, and you run against that force.

653
00:36:46,200 --> 00:36:51,860
But it really doesn't give you the same kind of loading on all of the bones that you would get

654
00:36:51,861 --> 00:36:58,740
merely from getting up and walking around as one would be in an ambulatory day here on Earth.

655
00:36:58,741 --> 00:37:01,640
But the use of exercise equipment in a weightless environment

656
00:37:01,740 --> 00:37:05,480
is only a partial solution.

657
00:37:05,580 --> 00:37:08,520
In the future, on a three-year manned voyage to Mars,

658
00:37:08,620 --> 00:37:11,180
better ways must be found to protect crew members

659
00:37:11,280 --> 00:37:12,880
against weightlessness.

660
00:37:12,980 --> 00:37:15,320
Certain drug therapies look promising.

661
00:37:15,420 --> 00:37:18,860
The kinds of drugs that we are looking at for preventing bone

662
00:37:18,960 --> 00:37:22,200
loss are the same kinds of drugs that the medical community is

663
00:37:22,300 --> 00:37:26,500
investigating for dealing with osteoporosis.

664
00:37:26,600 --> 00:37:28,240
Another possible countermeasure

665
00:37:28,241 --> 00:37:30,540
favored by the science fiction community,

666
00:37:30,640 --> 00:37:33,180
is artificial gravity.

667
00:37:33,280 --> 00:37:35,540
If you spin an entire spacecraft,

668
00:37:35,640 --> 00:37:38,920
centrifugal force will allow astronauts to live as if in real

669
00:37:39,020 --> 00:37:39,720
gravity.

670
00:37:42,780 --> 00:37:47,300
But spinning a large spaceship is not the way it will be done.

671
00:37:47,400 --> 00:37:48,800
It's too expensive.

672
00:37:48,900 --> 00:37:50,600
It takes too much mass.

673
00:37:50,700 --> 00:37:55,640
And it ignores some of the serious practical problems.

674
00:37:55,641 --> 00:37:59,340
However, the idea of artificial gravity,

675
00:37:59,440 --> 00:38:04,080
the idea of spinning a small part of the spacecraft

676
00:38:04,180 --> 00:38:08,880
to create a centrifugal force to substitute for gravitational

677
00:38:08,980 --> 00:38:11,880
force is a very, very good one.

678
00:38:11,980 --> 00:38:16,000
Research is pointing at the use of a small radius centrifuge,

679
00:38:16,100 --> 00:38:19,400
not as a substitute for Earth gravity 24-7,

680
00:38:19,500 --> 00:38:23,400
but as a sort of shot in the arm tonic for weightlessness.

681
00:38:23,401 --> 00:38:26,040
Another serious problem for deep space explorers

682
00:38:26,140 --> 00:38:29,940
is cosmic and solar radiation.

683
00:38:30,040 --> 00:38:32,080
These charged subatomic particles

684
00:38:32,180 --> 00:38:35,020
can go through the human body like a rifle bullet,

685
00:38:35,120 --> 00:38:38,220
shattering cells and eventually causing cancers.

686
00:38:38,320 --> 00:38:42,620
On the way to Mars, human survival depends, I believe,

687
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:46,260
on some innovative new approach to dealing

688
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:48,760
with the radiation issue.

689
00:38:48,860 --> 00:38:52,400
Drug therapies, again, could be the answer.

690
00:38:52,401 --> 00:38:54,940
Drugs helping women deal with radiation treatments

691
00:38:55,040 --> 00:38:57,340
for breast cancer might protect astronauts

692
00:38:57,440 --> 00:38:59,400
against the effects of cosmic radiation.

693
00:39:02,340 --> 00:39:05,240
During solar flare activity, deep space crews

694
00:39:05,340 --> 00:39:08,680
can wait out the radiation storm in a small, heavily shielded

695
00:39:08,780 --> 00:39:10,080
bunker on board the ship.

696
00:39:12,920 --> 00:39:15,460
A simpler and more natural approach to this problem

697
00:39:15,560 --> 00:39:17,960
may be to choose astronauts who have reached the prime

698
00:39:18,060 --> 00:39:19,060
of their life.

699
00:39:19,160 --> 00:39:21,660
There can be some advantages to flying older people

700
00:39:21,661 --> 00:39:25,900
in space, because after all, what ends up being of concern

701
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:29,460
in development of cancer is the accumulated lifetime

702
00:39:29,560 --> 00:39:31,800
radiation dose.

703
00:39:31,900 --> 00:39:34,400
Future technology for helping mankind survive

704
00:39:34,500 --> 00:39:37,800
in space and extreme environments is right here on

705
00:39:37,900 --> 00:39:39,320
Earth rapidly evolving.

706
00:39:42,120 --> 00:39:45,820
For some, nanotechnology, the making of machines invisible

707
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:48,760
to the human eye out of atoms and molecules,

708
00:39:48,860 --> 00:39:50,460
is where it's all happening.

709
00:39:50,461 --> 00:39:55,460
What we're trying to do is learn how to build machinery

710
00:39:55,560 --> 00:40:00,100
and materials at the molecular scale by picking up a molecule

711
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:03,600
from one place, carefully moving it and putting it down

712
00:40:03,700 --> 00:40:08,320
precisely where we want it to go to build the object that we're

713
00:40:08,420 --> 00:40:10,120
trying to build.

714
00:40:10,220 --> 00:40:12,820
Using a new breed of electron microscope,

715
00:40:12,920 --> 00:40:15,620
plans are being made to construct entire factories

716
00:40:15,720 --> 00:40:18,220
on atomic scales that will manufacture

717
00:40:18,221 --> 00:40:21,860
a variety of products.

718
00:40:21,960 --> 00:40:23,820
Nanomedical devices are envisioned

719
00:40:23,920 --> 00:40:25,960
that will cruise through the bloodstream,

720
00:40:26,060 --> 00:40:28,460
hunting down bacteria and viruses,

721
00:40:28,560 --> 00:40:31,160
repairing cell damage where needed.

722
00:40:31,260 --> 00:40:34,060
The respirocyte, a mechanical blood cell,

723
00:40:34,160 --> 00:40:38,080
would make an excellent addition to any survival kit.

724
00:40:38,180 --> 00:40:40,780
The basic idea for a respirocyte is very simple.

725
00:40:40,880 --> 00:40:45,320
You have a very small sphere, a tank of compressed oxygen,

726
00:40:45,321 --> 00:40:48,660
about a micron in size that could flow easily

727
00:40:48,760 --> 00:40:50,820
through your circulatory system.

728
00:40:50,920 --> 00:40:55,860
And it would let the oxygen out when it was needed

729
00:40:55,960 --> 00:41:00,960
and absorb and compress CO2.

730
00:41:01,060 --> 00:41:03,680
A diver taking a dose of respirocytes

731
00:41:03,780 --> 00:41:08,080
could hold his or her breath for hours at a time.

732
00:41:08,180 --> 00:41:11,220
But nanotechnology possibilities outside the body

733
00:41:11,320 --> 00:41:12,880
are just as exciting.

734
00:41:12,980 --> 00:41:15,320
Nanotechnology would let you make a survival suit.

735
00:41:15,321 --> 00:41:18,360
which would cover your skin much like a glove,

736
00:41:18,460 --> 00:41:21,800
would cover your hand, and it would provide insulation

737
00:41:21,900 --> 00:41:23,460
against hot and cold.

738
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:25,660
It would provide additional strength

739
00:41:25,760 --> 00:41:27,140
if you wanted to move.

740
00:41:27,240 --> 00:41:31,600
It would let you survive in a wide range of environments.

741
00:41:31,700 --> 00:41:33,440
In a sense, the suit would be alive

742
00:41:33,540 --> 00:41:37,000
with complex molecular machinery, constantly analyzing

743
00:41:37,100 --> 00:41:42,120
the environmental forces at play, mechanical blood cells

744
00:41:42,121 --> 00:41:45,520
that hold compressed air, nanomedical devices that hunt

745
00:41:45,620 --> 00:41:48,760
down bacteria and viruses.

746
00:41:48,860 --> 00:41:51,720
These are all in the realm of possibility,

747
00:41:51,820 --> 00:41:53,260
but not for decades to come.

748
00:41:57,860 --> 00:42:02,940
Extreme environments meet us with sublime indifference.

749
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:05,780
We have found in them formidable opponents

750
00:42:05,880 --> 00:42:09,480
that will never conquer, but can at least survive.

